<%#
  What a device sees when its link no longer works.

  It replaces a blank page. `head :not_found` was correct as a status and
  useless as an answer: the device reloads its bookmark, gets an empty body, and
  a care receiver is looking at a white screen with nothing on it — on the one
  screen in this product belonging to the person least able to work out what
  happened or what to do about it.

  Deliberately says nothing about *why*. A revoked link and an invented one get
  this same page, word for word, because telling somebody holding a dead link
  that it was once real is exactly the disclosure the identical-404 rule exists
  to prevent. "Ask the person who set this up" is true either way, and is the
  only useful instruction for somebody with no account to sign in to.
%>
<div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
  <div class="text-center">
    <p class="text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">This page isn't working</p>

    <p class="text-2xl text-gray-600 mb-8">
      Ask the person who set up your reminders to send you a new link.
    </p>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500">
      Your reminders are safe. Nothing has been lost.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
